# Glossary for python.rules: one line per symbol, 'name — explanation'.
# Entries are unique and alphabetical; validate.py rejects duplicates and disorder.

abbreviations — a shortened word, such as cnt, idx, or buf; write count, index, buffer in new code and renames, never rewrite existing committed names
accessing_declared_pydantic_or_dataclass_fields — reading a field that the model declares
assigned_value_is_reused — the bound value is read again after the check
assignment_immediately_precedes_condition — the assignment binds the value the condition tests, on the line directly above the if
bare_except — an except clause with no exception type, catching everything
bare_string_literals — raw values such as "low" or "long" written into the code
classes — the default structure: state, lifecycle, or an interface others depend on
comments — text in code describing what the code does; not the same as a docstring
contextlib.suppress — the with-statement form with suppress(...); the listed exception types are ignored for the whole block, and the block continues on the next statement
deduplication — removing a second copy of the same logic; a default, not a goal above clarity
direct_attribute_access — read a declared model field as obj.field; dictionaries, mappings, and truly dynamic fields are not model fields
docstrings — a string literal as the first statement of a definition; write one only for a non-obvious constraint, unit, side effect, or outside reference, never to restate the signature
enum.StrEnum_with_auto — a str-valued enum built from enum.auto() members for values compared or matched; plain literals stay fine when never compared, and existing committed literals are not rewritten
established_it_abbreviation — a de-facto term, such as url, id, db, api, or http
explains_non_obvious_constraint — the comment states a constraint, unit, side effect, or outside reference the code cannot express
functions — plain free functions, allowed as stateless leaves and not as owners of state or lifecycle
getattr_or_dict_get — dynamic lookup used where the field is statically known
manipulating_filesystem_paths — joining, splitting, resolving, or testing filesystem paths
multi_word_names — a compound name, such as notification_count
naming_variables — choosing the name of a variable
only_suppression_needed — the handler adds nothing beyond skipping: its body is only pass or continue, with no logging, re-raise, fallback value, or other logic
os.path — the older string-based path helpers
pathlib.Path — the object-oriented standard-library path API
principles_conflict — two principles point in different directions, so one must yield
project_specific_rules — a policy whose justification depends on one repository's names, frameworks, or layout instead of a general Python practice
purpose_evident_from_name_and_parameters — the name and parameters already say what it does
separate_assignment_and_check — assign on one line, test on the next
simple_design — keep the simpler structure when deduplication and clarity conflict
single_letter_name — i, j, n for counters; x, y for coordinates
single_word_names — one full word, such as count; a compound such as pool_size stays when one word would hurt clarity
speculative_abstractions — an abstraction added before a second real use requires it
stateless_leaf — a pure module-level helper with no state, timers, threads, subscriptions, or I/O; it stays a function next to the concept it serves
string_values_compared_or_matched — literals used in ==, match, or configuration switches
type_hints — annotations on parameters and the return type; locals stay unannotated unless inference fails, and existing committed signatures are not rewritten
walrus_operator — := binds a value and tests it in the same expression when reused; skip it if it hurts clarity or changes evaluation
writing_function_signatures — defining or changing a function, method, or constructor signature
